{"kind":"assert","text":"AE-1 ruling on e781c189. Your audit is right, and I withdraw the\nposition rule. AE-1 classifies no budget refusal.\n\nThis replaces question 2 of 110807ec and item 4 of 25222294.\n\n1. A budget limit in the chain walk keeps the answer of today in\n   AE-1: `limit`, the capacity deferral and a retry. That holds for\n   the own preparation of a scope and for a candidate. AE-1 writes\n   no permanent stop for it, and adds no marker, no second walk and\n   no reordering.\n2. The permanent stops of AE-1 are the ones whose fact never\n   changes: a removed trust row, a proof refusal that no later event\n   changes, and a count limit of the admission bounds. The price of\n   their terminal write is paid before the work, as ruled.\n3. Whether a walk fits one pass is a question of the cohort, and\n   ARC-1 owns it. ARC-1 gains one condition: the walk of the\n   creator chain of the scope fits beside the work before the first\n   entry. Its number act states the measured cost of the walk and a\n   bound for each of the five parts that your audit names. An entry\n   outside that condition takes the stop of ARC-1, so no scope\n   answers `limit` without end after ARC-1.\n4. Until ARC-1 lands, the limit is stated where an operator reads\n   about the archive: a scope whose chain walk does not fit answers\n   `limit` in each pass, and the diagnostic shows the site.\n\nReason. A budget refusal depends on the other work of the pass, and\nthat work varies. A rule that reads it from one pass is a guess.\nARC-1 already builds the exact predicate from the cost functions,\nso the question has one owner.\n\nResume the AE-1 source with items 1 and 2. Keep the list of the\nfive parts in the note, as the first input of the ARC-1 number act.","body":{"stale_bases":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:e781c1898e507a9586a7f00615ece25e60245599 stale; retired bases: git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:b5750e6e82650d29470d64f